A||||14||kempo||Argh!  Waterpump removal amoung other thigns...||||1||
Z||000000||kempo||12-15-2001||11:45 AM||||Any tips on removing it with the v6?  How long should I expect it to take?  What a PITA!  It's almost ready...  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||12-15-2001||11:53 AM||||Just make sure you make yourself a diagram as to where the bolts go when you remove them, they are of different sizes and lenghts. You might also need a puller to remove the pulley off the old pump to put on the new one. Get a good gasket sealer for the paper gaskets and makes sure you scrape off all the old gasket before putting the new one on. You also might need some thread sealer to help prevent leaks from the bolts.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||12-15-2001||02:18 PM||||It's easy to remove from the top - it just takes time. Remove the battery so you have room to work -- there are three different sizes of torx bolts and two studs (13mm I believe) -- to remove the pully from the old one use a long screwdriver or prybar between two bolts on the pully while braking the bolts loose (those are 13mm also) this is to keep the pully from turning while you break the bolts loose.<P>Remove the pully, remove the old pump, clean that area where the old gasket was, apply the new gasket to the new pump (I use a thin bead of RTV) and install the new pump -- should take about an hour (maybe longer if you haven't done one before)<P>Make sure you use a water pump with a metal impeller and not a plastic impeller or you will be changing it again next year!<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||FIERO1985||12-15-2001||03:29 PM||||I have done 3 myslef and it only gets easier, though I worked from top and bottom it was much easier. Pretty much just make sure you use a gasket along with some RTV (on bothe sides of mating surface) and get a great seal. <P><BR>Thats all,<BR>Dan<P>------------------<BR>1985 GT V6 <BR>"LETS RACE"||||reg||4
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||12-15-2001||03:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>It's easy to remove from the top - it just takes time. Remove the battery so you have room to work -- there are three different sizes of torx bolts and two studs (13mm I believe) -- to remove the pully from the old one use a long screwdriver or prybar between two bolts on the pully while braking the bolts loose (those are 13mm also) this is to keep the pully from turning while you break the bolts loose.<P>Remove the pully, remove the old pump, clean that area where the old gasket was, apply the new gasket to the new pump (I use a thin bead of RTV) and install the new pump -- should take about an hour (maybe longer if you haven't done one before)<P>Make sure you use a water pump with a metal impeller and not a plastic impeller or you will be changing it again next year!<P>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 12-15-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I use this method all the time. As a factual point, I did one yesterday afternoon. It wanted to be a PITA real bad but it just didn't have enough guts to win!<BR>1.5 hours from start work to start car.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||fldevel||12-15-2001||06:19 PM||||the one on my 85 GT has bolts and the one on my 88 formula uses a torx ..takes about 45 min .. i need to do the one on my formula now .. im not looking foreward to it .. PITA||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||12-16-2001||12:57 PM||||If you are talking about a V6 water pump you do not have to have the pully pressed off. (Thats for a 4 cyl pump) It is attached by 4 bolts.  <P>I would advise to remove the pully before you remove the belt. The belt will help keep the pulley from spinning too much if the bolts are tight.<P>What I would do with torx bolts is spray them with penetrating oil, let it soak through for about an hour and then carefully loosen them up. <P>Hope this helps out too! ||||reg||2
Z||000007||kempo||12-17-2001||08:04 PM||||CURSES!!  #%@^%$(*&^%(~~!!!!  I hate how a DEALER could/would/DID install this CHEAP, PLASTIC waterpump into my fiero less than 40,000 miles, before I owned the car.  GRRR!!  Anyway, here are the pics.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.bellatlantic.net/~vze245p8/fiero/pump1.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.bellatlantic.net/~vze245p8/fiero/pump3.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.bellatlantic.net/~vze245p8/fiero/pump4.jpg"> <BR>The next shot, I am pointing to where it completely cracked.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.bellatlantic.net/~vze245p8/fiero/pump5.jpg"> <BR>Ohh, the carnaige of it all!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.bellatlantic.net/~vze245p8/fiero/pump6.jpg"> <BR>NEVER GET A PLASTIC PUMP!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.bellatlantic.net/~vze245p8/fiero/pump7.jpg"> <P>Honest to God I've never ever in my life seen a pump that has had the fate of this one.  This will happen in your fiero with a plastic pump, I feel for you.  <BR>My mechanic estimated it a $300 job.  I said no thanks.  I managed to get a brand new pump for $40 w/lifetime warranty.  It took me about 4 hours to do.  <BR>I also want to say THANK YOU to the people that have helped contribute to this thread.  So, thanks!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.bellatlantic.net/~vze245p8/fiero/springme.jpg"> <BR>     <BR>0-60 6.9 seconds||||reg||13
Z||000008||tgowens||12-17-2001||08:35 PM||||Good job!! Great pics!! - now you know what we meant by the plastic/metal impeller thing.....||||reg||1
Z||000009||kempo||12-17-2001||08:57 PM||||tgowens- That is possibly the most disturbing thing I've ever seen.  I've never seen a plastic pump before.  It cracked a full 360º around the shaft.  I just couldn't believe it.  It was a real PITA.  The most annoying part was the factory fiberglass / insulant stuff that's on it, it really really irritates the heck out of your skin.  But, I did make sure to get the METAL waterpump, brand new, not rebuilt.  I am very surprised at how much better my heater works now.  I step on the accelerator, and not only do I go fast, heat just pours out of the vents.  Amen to that!  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||tgowens||12-17-2001||09:13 PM||||oops!! I knew I forgot something!! I forgot to tell you to wear long sleeves when you did that job!! I have changed lots of those pumps but I learned on the first one to wear long sleeves - I itched for two months from that insulation!!||||reg||1
Z||000011||88 Database Nut||12-17-2001||09:14 PM||||I had a plastic impeller water pump once. I explained my decision to give it a try in a post a while back.  Lasted about 1 year, then cracked radially across the shaft, about<BR>1 inch on either side.  Here's a tip to avoid the itchy arms.  Take a cheapo plastic garbage bag and cut two hand holes in the end and slip it over your arms.  That way, when you're in there dorking around with the bolts and scraping gaskets, the insulation won't be burying itself in your arms.  I actually haven't tried this, it was a suggestion from someone on the forum that sounds like a good thing.  Anyway, it's a PITA job but go get 'em!  Good Luck,<P>John Willhoff, Marietta GA<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Rodrv6||12-17-2001||10:05 PM||||I recently got rid of the plastic impeller pump in my 88 GT. Stupid design  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>My solution for the "itchy arm" problem is simple and permanent.  I just removed the insulation from both sides of the engine bay and left it out.  The engine bay is a lot cleaner looking and there is no difference in the sound level either inside or outside.  I left the piece on the firewall.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000013||kempo||12-17-2001||10:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Rodrv6:<BR><B>My solution for the "itchy arm" problem is simple and permanent.  I just removed the insulation from both sides of the engine bay and left it out.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is this okay to do?  I mean, some engineer put it there for a reason, and I've seen other people take it out before.  I figure it's there to absorb heat and protect stuff.  What are the effects of removing it, if any?  Also, what about the heat shield on the rear decklid, over the engine?  If I removed that, would my paint be affected by it?||||reg||5
Z||000014||tgowens||12-17-2001||11:17 PM||||I did the same thing with the insulation on my '87GT with the 3.4 (shoulda done it with my other GT's!) - it really makes no difference at all in the functionality of the vehicle. <P>Here's another tip to keep the fiberglass out of your arms (if you absolutely insist on working in short sleeves  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) - coat your arms with talcum powder before working around the insulation......<P>As for the plastic impeller pumps - the original pumps were plastic impellers and a lot of the replacements have been (a matter of cost) but to me, that was a major design flaw!! Every one I replace gets a metal impeller!||||reg||1
